      "id": 1,
      "text": "Davis City was founded in 1854 by Ohio native William Davis, who built a sawmill and the town was named in his honor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/davis_city_iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A flouring mill built in 1875 by the Clark family and the Davis City Elevator Company processed up to 80 carloads of grain annually in the early 1900s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/davis_city_iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1879 arrival of the Chicago, Burlington and Quincy Railroad turned Davis City into a vital shipping point for local agriculture and lumber.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        16,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/davis_city_iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1895 the town built the Davis City Park pavilion, a symbol of optimism that hosted social gatherings for decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/davis_city_iowa"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A USGS stream gauge on the Thompson River monitors water levels, helping farmers and residents keep track of flood risk.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
